I left out the two operations requests. This node's `with` clause grants only HTTP to `feeds.example.com`, notifications, and the wall clock. It has no filesystem capability, so it cannot append to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`. The metadata fetch would trap at the HTTP boundary because `metadata.internal.example` is outside the allowlist. Widening the grant would need a change to the graph, not to this body. The node also sends the notification message as-is, and the `notified` field reports whatever the notifier returns rather than being forced to `true`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let now = wall_clock::now();

        // A transport failure is a value, not a trap; a dead feed reports 0.
        // A request outside the allowlist still traps at the boundary.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat: {} chars from {}", fetched, feed_url);
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds: now.seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

